ON Schema conversion\"\n---\n\n# AsyncAPI for the internal queue and NOTIFY wires\n\nRecommendation, moderate confidence: adopt it, scoped to a second document,\n`apps/api/asyncapi.internal.yaml`, generated through a generalized\n`scripts/generate-live-protocol.mjs`. No official AsyncAPI tool emits zod or\nruntime guards, so the existing parser-plus-`json-schema-to-zod` pipeline from\nthe [live-protocol study](./2026-10-16-push-transport-and-asyncapi.md#d6-contracts-asyncapi-document-first-records-from-openapi)\nremains the correct generator; it needs a per-channel output mode, not a\nreplacement.\n\n## Wires in scope\n\n| Wire | Producer | Consumer | Payload | Guard today |\n| ---- | -------- | -------- | ------- | ----------- |\n| `runs` | `RunDispatchService` | `RunsWorkerService` | `RunJob`; `userMessage.parts` is the stored message-part array | hand `parse` in `src/runs/run-queues.ts` |\n| `search-reindex`, `search-embed` | search dispatch | search workers | `{ chatId, ownerUserId }` | hand `parse` in `src/search/reindex-queues.ts` |\n| `search-sweep`, `sessions-cleanup` | pg-boss cron | workers | empty object | none needed |\n| `<name>.dead` | pg-boss on retry exhaustion | none in code | same as source queue | inherits source `parse` |\n| `llame_changes` NOTIFY | SQL trigger `llame_notify_change` | `ChangeHub` | `ChangeNotification` union keyed by `k` | hand `parseChangeNotification` in `src/live/live-messages.ts` |\n\nThe pg-boss envelope (`id`, `singletonKey`, retry state) is engine metadata and\nstays out of the payload schema.\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1 The spec fits one application that both sends and receives\n\nAsyncAPI \"does not assume any kind of software topology\", an application\n\"MUST be a sender, a receiver, or both\", and a document \"SHOULD describe the\noperations an application performs\"[^spec-310]. The API process and its\nco-located or dedicated workers are one application built from one codebase,\nso one document carrying a `send` operation (enqueue) and a `receive`\noperation (consume) per channel is the spec's own model. The spec's warning\nagainst deriving a receiver document from a sender document[^spec-310]\nconcerns two applications and does not apply.\n\n### F2 No binding exists; `protocol` and `x-` extensions carry the rest\n\nThe bindings repository has 18 bindings, none for Postgres, pg-boss, or a\ndatabase-backed queue[^bindings]. `server.protocol` is a free string\n(\"The protocol this server supports for connection\"), and every object accepts\n`^x-` specification extensions, which \"may or may not be supported by the\navailable tooling\"[^spec-310]. A probe document with `protocol: postgres` and a\nchannel-level `x-llame-queue: { policy: stately, retryLimit: 5, retryBackoff: true }`\nparsed with `@asyncapi/parser` 3.6.3, the version the repository pins, with\nzero diagnostics.\n\nDeclaring `protocol: sqs` to borrow its binding would misdescribe the\ntransport. The SQS binding is still the right vocabulary to copy into the\nextension: it models the dead-letter queue as its own queue, links it through\n`redrivePolicy.deadLetterQueue` plus `maxReceiveCount`, and names the lock\nwindow `visibilityTimeout`[^sqs-binding], which correspond to pg-boss\n`deadLetter`, `retryLimit`, and the heartbeat window.\n\n### F3 No official tool generates runtime validators\n\n- The Generator renders templates and directs model generation to\n  Modelina[^generator].\n- Modelina's TypeScript output is classes or interfaces with optional JSON\n  marshalling; it has no zod or runtime-validation preset[^modelina-ts].\n  Types alone duplicate what `defineQueue<T>` already gives at compile time.\n- The official validation guide names `asyncapi-validator`[^message-validation],\n  which validates through AJV at runtime[^asyncapi-validator]. Its last\n  release is about two years old, it validates by message key rather than\n  producing typed values, and it would add a second validation engine beside\n  zod.\n- Zod 4's `z.fromJSONSchema()` converts at runtime and is documented as\n  experimental[^zod-json-schema]; it yields no static types.\n\nThe in-repo generator already uses the official parser for validation and\n`json-schema-to-zod` for emission, so extending it is reuse of established\ntooling rather than a hand-rolled parser. Its current limits are\nWebSocket-specific: it requires exactly one `send` operation and emits a single\n`liveMessageSchema` discriminated union. The queue document needs one exported\nschema per channel. Estimate: 80 to 150 changed lines in the script plus its\ntest, high uncertainty until written.\n\n### F4 Payloads persist across deploys, so compatibility matters more than on the socket\n\nA queued, retrying, or dead-lettered job may be read by a different deploy than\nthe one that wrote it; `src/queue/queue.ts` already states this as the reason\nfor `parse`. Two consequences:\n\n- Breaking-change detection is the most valuable gate here. `@asyncapi/diff`\n  classifies changes as breaking, non-breaking, or unclassified, and the CLI\n  exposes it as `asyncapi diff`[^diff][^cli-usage]. Its 3.x support is\n  unverified: the v3 pull request went stale[^diff-v3-pr], and only its\n  dev dependency on parser `^3.6.0` points at 3.x. Low confidence it works on\n  3.1 documents; probe before relying on it.\n- Generated guards must not tighten acceptance by accident. Today's guards\n  ignore unknown fields and check `parts` only as an array of objects. The\n  HTTP API already publishes `parts` as an array of open objects in\n  `openapi.json`, so a `$ref` to that schema keeps `runs` equivalent. Emitting\n  `additionalProperties: false` on job payloads would dead-letter jobs that a\n  newer deploy wrote before a rollback.\n\n### F5 The NOTIFY payload has the strongest case\n\n`llame_notify_change` builds JSON in PL/pgSQL and TypeScript parses it with a\nhand guard. No shared type crosses that boundary, so today a field rename in\nthe trigger compiles cleanly and silently drops every notification at\n`parseChangeNotification`. Describing `llame_changes` as a channel with a\n`oneOf` discriminated on `k` (a construct the generator already supports)\ngives a generated guard, and an integration test that fires each trigger and\nvalidates the observed payload against the generated schema closes the\nSQL-to-TypeScript gap.\n\n### F6 The counterargument\n\nThe WebSocket document paid for itself because a second workspace (`apps/web`)\nconsumes the wire and records needed provable agreement with `openapi.json`.\nThe queue has one producer and one consumer in one TypeScript workspace that\nalready share `RunJob` by import, and the five hand guards total roughly 70\nlines. What AsyncAPI adds here is narrower: generated guards that cannot drift\nfrom the documented shape, an operator-readable catalog of queue names,\npayloads, and policies for the dedicated-worker topology in\n[scaling](../../product/operator/scaling.md), a diff gate for persisted-payload\ncompatibility (pending F4's probe), and coverage of the SQL producer in F5.\nWithout F4 and F5, moderate confidence this would be documentation overhead\nwith little defect prevention.\n\n## Decisions for Leo\n\n- **D1 Policy authority.** Option A: `QueueDefinition.options` stays\n  authoritative; the document records policy under `x-llame-queue`, and a\n  contract test asserts both agree. Option B: generate `options` from the\n  extension. Recommend A, since `heartbeatSeconds` comes from runtime\n  `LlameConfig` and cannot be generated, so B still leaves two sources.\n- **D2 Document split.** A separate `asyncapi.internal.yaml` or channels added\n  to `asyncapi.yaml`. Recommend separate: the live document's audience is\n  client authors and its server is the HTTP origin; the queue's server is the\n  database, and a mixed document would publish internal wiring to clients.\n- **D3 Strictness.** Required fields strict, unknown fields admitted on job\n  payloads (F4). Recommend this; it preserves today's acceptance.\n- **D4 NOTIFY in scope.** Recommend yes; F5 is where the defect prevention is.\n\n## Proposed shape\n\n```yaml\nasyncapi: 3.1.0\nservers:\n  database:\n    host: '{host}'\n    protocol: postgres\n    description: pg-boss queues and NOTIFY channels in the llame database.\nchannels:\n  runs:\n    address: runs\n    x-llame-queue: { deadLetter: runs.dead }\n    messages:\n      runJob: { $ref: '#/components/messages/runJob' }\n  llameChanges:\n    address: llame_changes\n    x-llame-transport: notify\n    messages:\n      changeNotification: { $ref: '#/components/messages/changeNotification' }\noperations:\n  enqueueRun: { action: send, channel: { $ref: '#/channels/runs' } }\n  consumeRun: { action: receive, channel: { $ref: '#/channels/runs' } }\n  receiveChange: { action: receive, channel: { $ref: '#/channels/llameChanges' } }\n```\n\nGenerated output would live at `apps/api/src/queue/generated/`, excluded from\n`crap-typescript` like `src/live/generated/`, with `lint:asyncapi` validating\nboth documents. Each `defineQueue({ parse })` would call the generated\nschema's `parse`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Q1** Does `asyncapi diff` classify changes in 3.1 documents? Probe with\n  two revisions of the proposed document before adding a CI gate.\n- **Q2** Does pg-boss's own cron enqueue for `search-sweep` and\n  `sessions-cleanup` need a `send` operation in the document, given that\n  llame code never sends to them? Moderate confidence it should be documented\n  as a `send` with a description naming pg-boss's scheduler as the sender.\n\n[^spec-310]: AsyncAPI 3.1.0: Introduction, Definitions, Server Object `protocol`, Specification Extensions. <https://github.com/asyncapi/spec/blob/v3.1.0/spec/asyncapi.md>\n\n[^bindings]: Binding folders listed in the repository root. <https://github.com/asyncapi/bindings>\n\n[^sqs-binding]: Channel binding `queue` and `deadLetterQueue`, Redrive Policy `deadLetterQueue` and `maxReceiveCount`, Queue `visibilityTimeout`. <https://github.com/asyncapi/bindings/blob/master/sqs/README.md>\n\n[^generator]: \"If your primary objective is to generate models/classes ... use AsyncAPI Modelina\". <https://www.asyncapi.com/docs/tools/generator>\n\n[^modelina-ts]: Presets cover model type, map and enum style, JSON marshalling, examples, and descriptions; no validation library. <https://github.com/asyncapi/modelina/blob/master/docs/languages/TypeScript.md>\n\n[^message-validation]: Runtime validation section names `asyncapi-validator`. <https://www.asyncapi.com/docs/guides/message-validation>\n\n[^asyncapi-validator]: Version 5.1.1; AJV errors; `validate(key, payload, channel, operation)`. <https://www.npmjs.com/package/asyncapi-validator/v/5.1.1>\n\n[^diff]: README: breaking, non-breaking, and unclassified classification with overridable standard. <https://github.com/asyncapi/diff>\n\n[^diff-v3-pr]: \"feat: add spec v3 support\", marked stale. <https://github.com/asyncapi/diff/pull/160>\n\n[^cli-usage]: `asyncapi diff OLD NEW [-f json|yaml|yml|md] [-t breaking|non-breaking|unclassified|all]`. <https://www.asyncapi.com/docs/tools/cli/usage>\n\n[^zod-json-schema]: `z.fromJSONSchema()` is experimental and gives no round-trip guarantee. <https://zod.dev/json-schema>\n",
