            "command": "cd /home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/worktree-quiet-stone-b5b5; S=/tmp/claude-1000/-home-leon0399--herdr-worktrees-llame-worktree-quiet-stone-b5b5/6a35d8be-1a34-4187-96c3-63bcd3fd31ee/scratchpad; python3 - \"$S\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys,json,re\nS=sys.argv[1]\np='docs/research/realtime/2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=s.index('Recommendation, moderate confidence:')\nb=s.index('[^spec-310]: AsyncAPI 3.1.0:')\nbody='''Recommendation, moderate confidence: adopt it for the job queues as a second\ndocument, `apps/api/asyncapi.queues.yaml`, beside the WebSocket document\n`apps/api/asyncapi.websocket.yaml`, both generated through\n`scripts/generate-asyncapi-module.mjs`. No official AsyncAPI tool emits zod or\nruntime guards, so the parser-plus-`json-schema-to-zod` pipeline from the\n[live-protocol study](./2026-10-16-push-transport-and-asyncapi.md#d6-contracts-asyncapi-document-first-records-from-openapi)\nremains the generator; it gains a per-channel output mode. The NOTIFY payload\nis a candidate for the same treatment with a weaker case than first assumed\n(F5).\n\n## Wires in scope\n\n| Wire | Producer | Consumer | Payload | Guard before the queue document |\n| ---- | -------- | -------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `runs` | `RunDispatchService` | `RunsWorkerService` | `RunJob`; `userMessage.parts` is the stored message-part array | hand `parse` in `src/runs/run-queues.ts` |\n| `search-reindex`, `search-embed` | search dispatch | search workers | `{ chatId, ownerUserId }` | hand `parse` in `src/search/reindex-queues.ts` |\n| `search-sweep`, `sessions.cleanup` | pg-boss cron | workers | empty marker | none needed |\n| `<name>.dead` | pg-boss on retry exhaustion | none in code | same as source queue | inherits source `parse` |\n| `llame_changes` NOTIFY | SQL trigger `llame_notify_change` | `ChangeHub` | `ChangeNotification` union keyed by `k` | hand `parseChangeNotification` in `src/live/live-messages.ts` |\n\nThe pg-boss envelope (`id`, `singletonKey`, retry state) is engine metadata and\nstays out of the payload schema.\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1 The spec fits one application that both sends and receives\n\nAsyncAPI \"does not assume any kind of software topology\", an application\n\"MUST be a sender, a receiver, or both\", and a document \"SHOULD describe the\noperations an application performs\"[^spec-310]. The API process and its\nco-located or dedicated workers are one application built from one codebase,\nso one document carrying a `send` operation (enqueue) and a `receive`\noperation (consume) per channel is the spec's own model. The spec's warning\nagainst deriving a receiver document from a sender document[^spec-310]\nconcerns two applications and does not apply. A cron queue's `send` is\nperformed by pg-boss's scheduler; the operation's summary says so.\n\n### F2 No binding exists; `protocol` and `x-` extensions carry the rest\n\nThe bindings repository has 18 bindings, none for Postgres, pg-boss, or a\ndatabase-backed queue[^bindings]. `server.protocol` is a free string\n(\"The protocol this server supports for connection\"), and every object accepts\n`^x-` specification extensions, which \"may or may not be supported by the\navailable tooling\"[^spec-310]. A document with `protocol: postgres` and a\nchannel-level `x-llame-queue: { policy: stately, retryLimit: 5, retryBackoff: true }`\nparses with `@asyncapi/parser` 3.6.3, the version the repository pins, with\nzero diagnostics.\n\nDeclaring `protocol: sqs` to borrow its binding would misdescribe the\ntransport. The SQS binding is the closest standard vocabulary: it models the\ndead-letter queue as its own queue, links it through\n`redrivePolicy.deadLetterQueue` plus `maxReceiveCount`, and names the lock\nwindow `visibilityTimeout`[^sqs-binding], which correspond to pg-boss\n`deadLetter`, `retryLimit`, and the heartbeat window. `x-llame-queue` keeps\npg-boss's own option names instead, because they are what `QueueOptions`\npasses to the engine.\n\nA `$ref` from one multi-format schema to another fails the parser\n(`Property \"schemaFormat\" is not expected to be here`). The WebSocket\ndocument needs the OpenAPI format only to reference `openapi.json`; the queue\ndocument references nothing there and uses the default schema format.\n\n### F3 No official tool generates runtime validators\n\n- The Generator renders templates and directs model generation to\n  Modelina[^generator].\n- Modelina's TypeScript output is classes or interfaces with optional JSON\n  marshalling; it has no zod or runtime-validation preset[^modelina-ts].\n  Types alone duplicate what `defineQueue<T>` already gives at compile time.\n- The official validation guide names `asyncapi-validator`[^message-validation],\n  which validates through AJV at runtime[^asyncapi-validator]. Its registry\n  metadata was last modified on 2025-03-10, it validates by message key rather\n  than producing typed values, and it would add a second validation engine\n  beside zod.\n- Zod 4's `z.fromJSONSchema()` converts at runtime and is documented as\n  experimental[^zod-json-schema]; it yields no static types.\n\nThe in-repo generator uses the official parser for validation and\n`json-schema-to-zod` for emission. Its live mode requires exactly one `send`\noperation and emits a single discriminated union; the `--channels` mode emits\none `<channel>Channel` constant per channel carrying the address, the\n`x-llame-queue` options, and the payload schema.\n\n### F4 Payloads persist across deploys, so compatibility matters more than on the socket\n\nA queued, retrying, or dead-lettered job may be read by a different deploy than\nthe one that wrote it; `src/queue/queue.ts` states this as the reason for\n`parse`. Two consequences:\n\n- `@asyncapi/diff` 0.5.0 does not detect payload breaks. It runs on 3.1\n  documents, but adding a required field to a payload is reported as\n  `unclassified` on every path, with nothing under `breaking`\n  ([probe output](./2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi-diff-probe.json)). The\n  CLI wrapper `asyncapi diff`[^cli-usage] requires Node 24 in `@asyncapi/cli`\n  6.2.0, above the repository's Node 22 pin. A compatibility gate would have\n  to be llame's own rule over the payload schemas.\n- Generated guards must not tighten acceptance by accident. The hand guards\n  ignored unknown fields and checked `parts` only as an array of objects.\n  Leaving `additionalProperties` unset makes the generated zod objects strip\n  unknown keys, which keeps that acceptance, so a job written by a newer\n  deploy survives a rollback. `z.number().int()` in zod 4 enforces the safe\n  integer range the hand guard checked for `seq`.\n\n### F5 The NOTIFY payload is covered end to end only in part\n\n`llame_notify_change` builds JSON in PL/pgSQL and TypeScript parses it with a\nhand guard, so no shared type crosses that boundary. The gateway integration\ntest drives a chat rename and a Run through the database and asserts the\nsocket messages, so a trigger-side rename of the `chat` or `run` fields fails\nCI. The `project`, `pin` and `run_event` variants are exercised only by unit\ntests over hand-built payloads. Describing `llame_changes` as a channel with a\n`oneOf` discriminated on `k` would make the contract declarative and cover\nevery variant uniformly; the defect it prevents is narrower than for the\nqueues.\n\n### F6 The counterargument\n\nThe WebSocket document paid for itself because a second workspace (`apps/web`)\nconsumes the wire and records needed provable agreement with `openapi.json`.\nThe queues have one producer and one consumer in one TypeScript workspace that\nalready shared `RunJob` by import, behind roughly 70 lines of hand guards.\nWhat AsyncAPI adds is narrower: guards that cannot drift from the documented\nshape, queue names and static options taken from the same document, and an\noperator-readable catalog of queue payloads and policies for the\ndedicated-worker topology in [scaling](../../product/operator/scaling.md).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **D1 Policy authority.** The document is authoritative for static options:\n  each queue definition takes `options` from its generated channel constant.\n  Options from operator configuration (the runs heartbeat window) and the runs\n  job expiry stay in code and are described in the channel's `description`.\n- **D2 Document split.** Two documents with explicit names. The WebSocket\n  document's audience is client authors and its server is the HTTP origin;\n  the queue document's server is the database.\n- **D3 Strictness.** Required fields strict, unknown fields ignored (F4).\n- **D4 NOTIFY.** Deferred: the case rests on the three variants without\n  end-to-end coverage (F5).\n\n'''\ns=s[:a]+body+s[b:]\n# drop diff-v3-pr source + footnote\ns=re.sub(r'  - id: diff-v3-pr\\n.*\\n.*\\n','',s)\ns=re.sub(r'\\n\\[\\^diff-v3-pr\\]:.*\\n','\\n',s)\ns=re.sub(r'\\n\\[\\^diff\\]:.*\\n','\\n',s)\ns=re.sub(r'  - id: diff\\n.*\\n.*\\n','',s)\ns=s.replace('title: \"AsyncAPI for the internal queue and NOTIFY wires\"','title: \"AsyncAPI for the job queue and NOTIFY wires\"')\ns=s.replace('# AsyncAPI for the internal queue and NOTIFY wires','# AsyncAPI for the job queue and NOTIFY wires')\ns=s.replace('description: \"Assesses describing llame\\'s pg-boss job payloads and the llame_changes NOTIFY payload in an AsyncAPI 3.1 document, generating their runtime guards with the existing live-protocol generator, and which AsyncAPI tooling applies.\"','description: \"Assesses describing llame\\'s pg-boss job payloads and the llame_changes NOTIFY payload in AsyncAPI 3.1, which AsyncAPI tooling applies, and why the queue guards are generated by the repository\\'s own parser-plus-zod generator.\"')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nd=json.load(open(f'{S}/diffprobe/diff.log'))\njson.dump({\"tool\":\"@asyncapi/diff 0.5.0 with @asyncapi/parser 3.6.3\",\"change\":\"search-embed payload gains a required string field `extra`\",\"result\":d},open('docs/research/realtime/2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi-diff-probe.json','w'),indent=2)\np='docs/research/realtime/index.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"- [AsyncAPI for the internal queue and NOTIFY wires](./2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi.md) - Assesses describing llame's pg-boss job payloads and the llame_changes NOTIFY payload in an AsyncAPI 3.1 document, generating their runtime guards with the existing live-protocol generator, and which AsyncAPI tooling applies.\",\"- [AsyncAPI for the job queue and NOTIFY wires](./2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi.md) - Assesses describing llame's pg-boss job payloads and the llame_changes NOTIFY payload in AsyncAPI 3.1, which AsyncAPI tooling applies, and why the queue guards are generated by the repository's own parser-plus-zod generator. Data: [diff probe](./2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi-diff-probe.json).\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\nhead -3 docs/research/realtime/index.md; rg -n \"diff\\]|diff-v3|\\^diff\" docs/research/realtime/2026-10-17-queue-wire-asyncapi.md"
